    "c1": {
      "anchor": "q1",
      "kind": "item",
      "label": "Will you push the netlists branch for this epic to merge, or should it rebuild the column features on main?",
      "excerpt": "Will you push the netlists branch for this epic to merge, or should it rebuild the column features on main?",
      "text": "I'll give you the components it built in a second.  You won't get the actual netlist stuff, work without it.",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-19T03:13:12.849Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-19T03:15:00.546Z",
          "html": "<p>Got it. When you hand over the components (column mode for <code>&lt;ui-tree&gt;</code>, and <code>&lt;ui-graph&gt;</code>), P1 brings them in onto <code>main</code>'s column view. No netlist code or netlist files come across. Every netlist step works from a small made-up example, never real netlist data. The plans are being changed to match.</p>"
        }
      ]
    },
    "c2": {
      "anchor": "p-file-upload-in-spell",
      "kind": "phase",
      "label": "File Upload In Spell",
      "excerpt": "File Upload In Spell",
      "text": "lets add a `destination` URL attr for where the results would be posted, and an `action` for spell action to invoke instead.\n\nAlso, size limit, max file count.  Anything else that uploader thingers usually show?  What would the UI look like for this?",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-19T23:26:14.665Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-19T23:28:08.310Z",
          "html": "<p>P9's plan is rewritten: <code>upload</code> is now two attributes, <code>destination</code> (a URL each file is posted to) and <code>action</code> (a Spell action called with the files instead). Both set: the action wins and a warning is logged. Neither: the files are only held and listed, and <code>ui-upload</code> still fires first, so a handler can take any upload over.</p>\n<p>Added <code>max-size</code> and <code>max-files</code>, and the plan now says what it looks like: a drop area with a Choose files button and the limits, then a row per file with its size, a progress bar, a tick or the reason it failed, and an ✕.</p>\n<p>One clash to know: in plain HTML a form's <code>action</code> is the URL it posts to, and <code>&lt;ui-input&gt;</code>'s <code>action</code> places its joined buttons. The plan keeps your name and says in the docs that it's a Spell action, not a URL.</p>"
        },
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "at": "2026-11-19T23:34:01.214Z",
          "text": "Sounds good.  Would be good it the uploader sent `file-drag*`, `file-drop*` and `folder-*` events that could be caught by the page, e.g. to update the UI.  \n\nThese should have all of the available data (filename, size, OSFileHandle) and the same events should be sent if the drop happened from showing the file open dialog, or from drag-and-drop onto the page/section.\n\nAlso, <ui-uploader> should have a `page` attribute (push back if the name is unclear) which says \"dropping anywhere on the page is the same as dropping in this component.\"  Unclear what should happen if that is turned on for multiple uploaders on the page.",
          "edited": "2026-11-19T23:37:23.655Z"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-19T23:37:44.023Z"
      },
      "working": "2026-11-19T23:37:44.023Z"
    },
    "c3": {
      "anchor": "p-subclass-elements-in-spell#list-1",
      "kind": "list",
      "label": "Subclass Elements In Spell",
      "excerpt": "A plain view: a \"solitaire\" is a view . A view on a built-in base, the closest m",
      "text": "where is the code for this?",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-19T23:30:11.356Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "Two sided view\" is a built-in template",
      "offset": 128,
      "edited": "2026-11-19T23:30:16.156Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-19T23:30:45.492Z",
          "html": "<p>It's written in Spell itself, then compiled once into the core:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The template: <code title=\"parser/src/core/templates/TwoSidedView.spell\">TwoSidedView.spell</code>, beside its Spanish twin, <code title=\"parser/src/core/templates/TwoSidedView.es.spell\">TwoSidedView.es.spell</code>. It starts <code>a \"two sided view\" is a view</code>, with its slots front and back, its direction, <code>to turn it over</code>, and its drawing and CSS.</li>\n<li>The list of built-in templates (two sided view, site, page layout): <code title=\"parser/src/spell/builtInTemplates.ts\">builtInTemplates.ts</code>.</li>\n<li><code>yarn core:gen:templates</code> compiles each one into the core class <code title=\"parser/src/core/classes/TwoSidedView.ts\">TwoSidedView.ts</code>, and its declarations into <code title=\"parser/src/spell/builtInTemplates.generated.ts\">builtInTemplates.generated.ts</code>.</li>\n<li>So <code>a card is a two sided view</code> compiles to just the card's own members, and extends the core's <code>TwoSidedView</code>. P11 would do the same thing, with a Spell UI element as the base.</li>\n</ul>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-19T23:30:32.039Z"
      }
    },
    "c4": {
